      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-15-wonder-scores-a-425-million-partnership-with-doordash-as-it-",
      "title": "Wonder scores a $425 million partnership with DoorDash as it builds its food empire",
      "summary": "Founded by veteran e-commerce entrepreneur Marc Lore, Wonder is slowly building a massive portfolio of restaurants and delivery companies.",
      "body": "The delivery platform DoorDash is investing in and partnering with Marc Lore-owned food tech company Wonder in deals worth a total of $425 million, the company announced on Tuesday. DoorDash will acquire Wonder’s Grubhub Campus Dining business (formerly Tapingo) for $300 million and will add $125 million to Wonder’s $650 million Series D round, announced in July.\nA chain of ghost kitchen food halls designed for delivery, Wonder is trying to take a one-size-fits-all approach to fast casual dining, allowing customers to order food from a variety of different restaurants, all under the same roof. Wonder, founded by veteran e-commerce entrepreneur Marc Lore, whose prior companies were acquired by Amazon and Walmart, is slowly building a takeout empire.\nLast year, Wonder acquired the food delivery platform Grubhub for $650 million, adding to its portfolio of brands like Blue Apron and Tastemade. Wonder also bought the New York City-based restaurant chains Mighty Quinn’s BBQ, Salt Hank’s, and Blue Ribbon Fried Chicken.\nLore said in a statement that the DoorDash partnership allows Wonder to “focus on [its] long-term ambition: a fully autonomous food system that can plan, produce and deliver personalized meals at scale, serving all 21 meals people eat each week.”\nWith its acquisition, DoorDash said that it wants to expand Wonder’s Grubhub Campus Dining business, which is live at 450 colleges and universities, to stadiums, hotels, and other venues.\n“Adding Grubhub Campus Dining to our platform will enable us to bring greater choice and convenience to college students while streamlining operations and reducing friction for universities,” DoorDash co-founder Tony Xu said in a statement.\nFor those who live in the Northeast or Mid-Atlantic regions, the fast growth of Wonder is palpable. It seems as though new stores are popping up all over the place, and it’s not an illusion. Wonder says it now has 157 locations, more than quadrupling its footprint since the start of 2025. It plans to expand to Texas in early 2027.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nAmanda Silberling is a senior writer at TechCrunch covering the intersection of technology and culture. She has also written for publications like Polygon, MTV, the Kenyon Review, NPR, and Business Insider. She is the co-host of Wow If True, a podcast about internet culture, with science fiction author Isabel J. Kim. Prior to joining TechCrunch, she worked as a grassroots organizer, museum educator, and film festival coordinator. She holds a B.A. in English from the University of Pennsylvania and served as a Princeton in Asia Fellow in Laos.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Amanda by emailing amanda@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at @amanda.100 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Amanda Silberling",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-15T15:21:10.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/wonder.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/15/wonder-scores-a-425-million-partnership-with-doordash-as-it-builds-its-food-empire/",
