      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-15-show-hn-pizza-bot-an-inbox-for-ai-agents-that-work-in-the-ba",
      "title": "Show HN: Pizza Bot – An inbox for AI agents that work in the background",
      "summary": "Hi HN - long-time lurker (since 2012!), first time poster.\nPizza Bot is a self-hosted desktop app for Mac, Windows, and Linux that runs AI agents in the background and exposes them through an email-like UI. Finished work shows up in Unread, and anything waiting on your approval shows up in Action. It's Apache 2.0-licensed, there's no signup and no telemetry, and you bring your own model provider: ",
      "body": "Pizza Bot is an inbox for long-running AI work. Start or schedule a task, return\nto your day, and let completed work collect in Unread while runs waiting for\nyour decision collect in Action. Agents keep working when you navigate away\nor disconnect; the api-server process must remain running.\nPizza Bot uses a stateful DeepAgents/LangGraph runtime with the same React\nexperience in Electron and the browser. The desktop app, web app, and terminal\nCLI all communicate with the api-server over HTTP/SSE.\nPizza Bot was developed at Amazon and is released under the Apache 2.0 license.\nWork asynchronously. Switch conversations without stopping their runs.\nReturn to the right queue. Completed work lands in Unread; durable approval\nOrganize conversations. Group threads into folders without hiding matching\nwork from the global Unread and Action queues.\nResume real work. Checkpointed runs survive client disconnects, and cron or\nwebhook triggers can start work without an open conversation.\nDelegate to specialists. Skills become tool-scoped subagents whose progress\nappears in the Activity panel.\nBring your model provider. Amazon Bedrock, Anthropic, Google Gemini,\nOpenAI, OpenRouter, and Ollama are supported.\nKeep control of consequential actions. Human-in-the-loop approvals,\nlong-term memory, file attachments, and desktop notifications are built into\nGrant local access explicitly. Add individual read-only or writable folders\nunder Settings > Files; Pizza Bot receives no default home-directory access.\nInstallers for macOS (Intel and Apple silicon), Windows, and Linux (x64 and\narm64) are attached to every release, with a SHA256SUMS to\ncheck a download against. The macOS builds are signed and notarized; the Linux\npackages are not signed, so verify them against the checksums.\nNode.js 24 or newer is required.\nnpm run dev starts the Vite frontend and Electron desktop shell. The shell\nforks and supervises its own api-server, matching the packaged application's\nprocess model. Configure a model under Settings > Providers before starting\nSee Running from source for isolated data roots, browser and\nCLI development, desktop packages, and remote backends.\nLocal inbox with an embedded backend\nWeb development or static deployment\nScripts, terminals, and remote backends\nRemote Electron, browsers, containers, or Linux services\nA running api-server needs access to at least one model provider; HTTP clients\ndo not. Configure Amazon Bedrock, Anthropic, Google Gemini, OpenAI, OpenRouter,\nor Ollama in Settings > Providers. Bedrock accepts an AWS profile, AWS\naccess keys, or a Bedrock API key, with an optional region override; otherwise\nAWS_REGION or us-west-2 is used. Bedrock combines its native catalog with\nthe regional Mantle catalog and routes models through Converse, OpenAI\nResponses or Chat Completions, or Anthropic Messages according to their\nOpenAI and Anthropic also accept custom base URLs for compatible endpoints;\nOpenAI can explicitly select Responses or Chat Completions, and Anthropic\nsupports x-api-key or bearer authentication. Select a model with\nPIZZA_MODEL=<provider>:<id>. The desktop protects entered secrets with\nElectron safeStorage; server configuration persists only environment-variable\nAdd MCP servers from the UI or <PIZZA_DATA_ROOT>/.mcp.json. Add Agent Skills\nunder <PIZZA_DATA_ROOT>/skills, or install plugins that package MCP servers and\nskills together. Skills become available after their declared tools are enabled\nand connected. A custom skill can replace a Built-in or Plugin skill with the\nsame id without modifying the original; removing the customization reveals the\nBuilt-in or Plugin version again. The Built-in Pizza Bot Guide can explain\nfeatures, suggest workflows, help with setup, and point to project documentation.\nSee Extending Pizza Bot for configuration, environment\nreferences, skill authoring, approval gates, and plugin installation.\napps/        api-server (Hono) | cli | desktop-shell (Electron) | web (React)\npackages/    core | runtime-langgraph | inference-providers | plugin-api | plugin-sdk | storage | logging\nplugins/     bundled Plugin packages and their packaging workspace\nskills/      optional Built-in Agent Skills\ntests/       LangGraph compatibility and protocol conformance\nThe production graph engine is isolated to packages/runtime-langgraph;\nfrontends consume protocol projections rather than importing runtime or model\nRunning - desktop, browser, CLI, and package commands.\nExtending - MCP servers, skills, and plugins.\nArchitecture - system boundaries, event model,\npersistence, transports, and design decisions.\nStandalone backend - authentication, remote\nElectron, static browser deployment, Docker, Compose, and Kubernetes.\nContributing - development setup, CI checks, worktrees,\nSecurity - network defaults, credentials, local data, and\nLogging - diagnostics, retention, viewing, and redaction.\nRoadmap - exploratory directions and the principles used to\nCode of Conduct - community participation\nLocal-first by default. The api-server binds to 127.0.0.1; non-loopback\nbinding requires authentication and an explicit origin allowlist.\nApplication state stays local. Threads, checkpoints, memories,\nattachments, and logs live under <PIZZA_DATA_ROOT>\n(~/.pizza-bot-oss by default). Model and tool requests go to the providers\nLocal folders require an explicit grant. Each folder added under\nSettings > Files is read-only unless you allow writes. Remote grants name\npaths on the backend host.\nMCP servers and plugins are trusted. Their commands and materializers can\nexecute with your user account's permissions. Install only sources you trust.\nSee SECURITY.md for the complete security model and vulnerability\nPizza Bot was designed, built, and brought into the open by its Executive Chefs\nPizza Bot was also shaped by more than 2,000 users across Amazon who tested\nearlier versions and shared feedback from real-world use. Their bug reports,\nideas, and candid input helped make Pizza Bot ready for a broader community.\nThank you to everyone who contributed.\nApache-2.0. See NOTICE for attribution notices.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "jd_",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-15T15:20:26.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://opengraph.githubassets.com/92e308a9bf3efe5a3b154aec4b6a4ea4997bb4314cf80e988775ed8c65905516/pizza-bot-app/pizza-bot",
      "originalUrl": "https://github.com/pizza-bot-app/pizza-bot",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-15-now-light-s-minimalist-phone-can-easily-call-an-uber",
      "title": "Now Light’s minimalist phone can easily call an Uber",
      "summary": "More than a decade after its Kickstarter proposed a \"cell phone designed to be used as little as possible,\" Light is adding a direct connection to Uber and Lyft with a new Rideshare tool. For owners of either the Light Phone III or the upcoming Light Flip, they can use it to find pick up / drop off locations, see fares and ETA, and select different trip options or saved locations they frequently v",
      "body": "More than a decade after its Kickstarter proposed a “cell phone designed to be used as little as possible,” Light is adding a direct connection to Uber and Lyft with a new Rideshare tool. For owners of either the Light Phone III or the upcoming Light Flip, they can use it to find pick up / drop off locations, see fares and ETA, and select different trip options or saved locations they frequently visit. They can use it to book the ride, and then see information about the driver and ETA, and call the driver if necessary.\nIt’s an early preview of the LightOS “tool library” SDK that will allow developers to expand the phones’ features while still promising to minimize feeds and notifications. A blog post from Light says that “We’ll open up user submissions for the Tool Library by the end of this month, and look forward to having those available to everyone a few weeks after that!”\nThe rideshare integration works via a partnership with Tremp, which already operates an SMS-based way to order rideshares, and users set up access via Light’s dashboard website, just like other LightOS tools. It’s not free to use, however, as Tremp charges a 10 percent service fee with a $2 minimum, but it will waive that fee for the first five rides within 30 days of creating an account.\nThe pictures show all the necessary features operating, although we will have to see it in operation to find out if there are any hang-ups. The expansion of app-like features on a minimalist smartphone might seem counterintuitive, but Light cofounder Kaiwei Tang has addressed that previously, saying, “I’m not trying to design vintage phones… I want to design all this modern technology, from the ground up, and eliminate all the bullshit.”\nThat’s why adding one feature users absolutely need access to — whether it’s a way to call for a ride, access to Spotify playlists, or something else — might be a way to keep them from swapping back to another device with more algorithms and alerts that constantly pop up.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "Lyft",
        "News",
        "Phones",
        "Ride-sharing",
        "Transportation",
        "Uber"
      ],
      "author": "Richard Lawler",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-15T15:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Rideshare.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,20.310681074267,100,34.48257725788",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/gadgets/995449/light-phone-uber-lyft-rideshare-app",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-15-meta-8217-s-new-one-subscriptions-put-a-price-on-social-medi",
      "title": "Meta&#8217;s new One subscriptions put a price on social media and AI",
      "summary": "Shortly after launching its new do-everything AI assistant Muse, Meta's launching subscription bundles that pair its standalone app subscriptions with extra AI usage. Some of the new Meta One bundles were in testing earlier this year, but are now available globally starting today, with several tiers for individual users, creators, and businesses. Meta says the \"core experience\" on its apps and Met",
      "body": "Shortly after launching its new do-everything AI assistant Muse, Meta’s launching subscription bundles that pair its standalone app subscriptions with extra AI usage. Some of the new Meta One bundles were in testing earlier this year, but are now available globally starting today, with several tiers for individual users, creators, and businesses. Meta says the “core experience” on its apps and Meta AI will still be free, and users can still get its subscriptions for Facebook, Instagram, and WhatsApp without a bundle. It also says it plans to expand the bundles to include “Edits, AI glasses, and more over time.”&nbsp;\nThere are two bundles for individual users, Core for $7.99 per month and Premium for $19.99 per month. They include Instagram Plus, WhatsApp Plus, and Facebook Plus, with “more media generation with Meta AI,” like generating images with Muse or using in-app AI features like Instagram’s Restyle. That’s a few dollars cheaper than paying $11 per month for all three of the standalone subscriptions.&nbsp;&nbsp;\nThe Meta One subscriptions aimed at creators and businesses range from $14.99 to $499 per month. They also include Meta’s individual app subscriptions, along with perks like a bold follow button, a verified badge, higher rankings in search results, and monitoring for fake impersonation accounts:&nbsp;\n* Essential ($14.99/month): Establish yourself and build confidence with new audiences. Get tools to manage your presence and more access to Meta Business Agent on WhatsApp to respond to customers 24/7. Plus, a verified badge, verified channel on WhatsApp Business app, and impersonation protection so people know you’re who you say you are (pending successful verification).\n* Advanced ($49.99/month): Invest in professional-grade tools. Advanced publishing features like scheduling stories up to 30 days in advance, links in organic posts and reels, exportable analytics, and deeper audience insights. Give team members access to your account without sharing your password and get more linked devices and business broadcast credits on WhatsApp. Plus, even more Meta Business Agent responses.\n* Expert ($149/month) and Max ($499/month): Scale and optimize. These plans include our highest levels of feature access and Meta Business Agent capacity for teams managing at scale.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Facebook",
        "Instagram",
        "Meta",
        "News",
        "Social Media",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Stevie Bonifield",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-15T15:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Meta-One-Bundle-plans-for-individuals.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,0,100,93.063453493324",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/995453/meta-one-subscriptions-ai",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-15-an-inquest-verdict-at-last-but-many-mysteries-remain-about-n",
      "title": "An inquest verdict at last - but many mysteries remain about Noah Donohoe's death",
      "summary": "After years of delay, the jury has reached a verdict but some questions remain unanswered over what happened to the 14-year-old.",
      "body": "An inquest verdict at last - but many mysteries remain about Noah Donohoe's deathByKevin SharkeyBBC News NIReporting fromat Belfast's Coroner's CourtIt's been more than six years since teenager Noah Donohoe was found dead 600m into a freezing underground water tunnel in Belfast.His death – and the speculation over what happened to him in the week between his daylight disappearance and the discovery of his body – became a huge news story that provoked countless unanswered questions.Now, after an inquest and months of exhaustive hearings and long deliberations by a jury, there is a verdict – but much is still unknown about what happened to Noah.Why, the night before he disappeared, did he leave his home in the middle of the rain-soaked night? Where did he go? Why did he come back 34 minutes later, as documented by CCTV, barefoot?As it happened: Noah Donohoe inquest jury finds failings by department officials possibly contributed to deathHours later, on Sunday 21 June 2020, he left the south Belfast home he shared with his mother for the final time.Why did he leave and go to an unfamiliar part of north Belfast? Why was the teenager naked when he was last seen in the area of the water tunnel entrance?And what might have been if he had seen the social media message sent him by one of two friends he planned to meet in north Belfast telling him their plans were cancelled?An autopsy found Noah had died from drowning, with the tunnel he was found in heading towards Belfast Lough and its tides.Now, after years of delays, a jury has found that it was possible, but not probable, that failings by the Department of Infrastructure over the tunnel in which he was found contribute to his death.They also found 13 failings by the Police Service of Northern Ireland (PSNI) but that these did not in any way contribute to the 14-year-old's death.Having spent months in court following all the evidence, it was clearly not an easy process for the men and women of the jury.They heard from dozens of witnesses and received voluminous amounts of official and expert documentation.This week they deliberated on Monday and Tuesday. But it came more than two months after the inquest was dramatically adjourned in the early hours after three days of deliberations did not lead to a verdict.Image source, Coroner's CourtWhile these findings will help his family and the community to understand a little bit more about what happened after Noah went missing, there is no certainty about many of the mysteries surrounding some of the unusual and extraordinary events in the lead-up to his disappearance.It may be that there will never be definitive answers to many of those questions.But one thing was very clear at the inquest – there was no evidence of any physical interaction between Noah and anyone else during his final bicycle ride from his home to north Belfast.During his summing up, the coroner Mr Justice Rooney ruled out several possibilities, including drugs, foul play, suicide, or a psychotic experience.He told the jury before they began deliberations that there was no evidence to support such findings and their verdict had to be based \"solely on the evidence you have heard and seen in this court\".There was no evidence of drugs in Noah's body during the postmortem.Image source, Coroner's CourtImage caption, Noah's journey from south to north Belfast was captured on a series of CCTV camerasBut the inquest heard expert witness evidence that explained, in some cases, drugs can degrade in a body after a few days, so the possibility of drugs having been in his system \"cannot be completely excluded\".The inquest also heard no evidence of third-party involvement or any link to loyalist paramilitaries, at odds with years of endless social media speculation that sometimes leaned into conspiracies.Many phases of the inquest focused on next-of-kin concerns about the Police Service of Northern Ireland's (PSNI) investigation into Noah's disappearance, as well as public safety around the tunnel system where Noah's body was found. The tunnel is maintained by Stormont's Department for Infrastructure.The police accepted that they had missed some investigative opportunities and have since apologised to Noah's mum, Fiona Donohoe.And throughout it all, day after day, Fiona Donohoe was a constant, quietly dignified presence, often sitting alone in a corner at the back of the Coroner's Court.A campaign, delays and coroner changeImage source, PacemakerImage caption, Noah's mother Fiona Donohoe attended every day of the inquest The road to the inquest was a long one for Fiona and her family.They mounted a high-profile public campaign around Noah's death, with dozens of supporters soon growing to hundreds and then thousands under the umbrella name of Noah's Army.They took their campaign to the streets in the form of cavalcades around Belfast, as well as protests at Stormont's Parliament Buildings and at PSNI headquarters.Frustration at the police came to a head in August 2022, when thousands of campaigners protested outside Belfast City Hall over the PSNI's decision to seek a public interest immunity (PII) certificate in relation to the case.As the years went on, the pre-inquest process was plagued by delays.The original coroner in the case stepped aside and the Donohoe family made a request for the inquest to be heard in front of a jury.Finally, five-and-a-half years after he disappeared, the inquest into Noah's death began in January 2026.What happened the night before Noah disappeared?Questions about Noah's mindset and behavioural changes before he went missing featured prominently at the inquest.The inquest heard how the schoolboy went out onto streets beside his home in the Holylands area of Belfast in the early hours of the day of his disappearance.Shortly after 03:30 BST, Noah walked out of his home into the rain.He was alone and wearing light summer clothing, including shorts and flip flops. He also had a set of headphones.Image caption, CCTV footage showed Noah leaving his home in the early hours of the day of his disappearanceDuring the following 34 minutes, some of his movements were captured on CCTV as he walked along nearby streets barefoot.When he returned home, shortly after 04:00 BST, the flip flops he was wearing were missing, he no longer had his headphones and a belt on his shorts was hanging loose.The inquest heard from one witness who examined the CCTV footage and the hearing explored the possibility there may have been the \"shape of something\" inside one of the pockets of his shorts when he returned home.But there was no evidence to explain what might have been in his pocket.The inquest was also unable to find out why he left his home in the darkness, or if he went to any property or met anyone while he was away from his home in the early hours of that morning.What happened the day of Noah Donohoe's disappearance?About 12 hours later, Noah was preparing to leave home again, this time to meet his school friends Charlie Rocks and Jay Tierney.They were part of a close group of friends at St Malachy's College, who Jay affectionately described to the inquest as \"very nerdy kids\".He told the court Noah was \"the most intelligent human being I have ever met\", while Charlie said he was \"the smartest kid, always asking questions\".The three were to meet up after a long period of Covid-enforced lockdown, with plans made to go to Cave Hill, a country park in the north of the city.Noah cycled away from his home off the Lower Ormeau Road at about 17:40, messaging one of his friends a few minutes before he left to check all was going to plan.\"Ok the cav?\" he asked, before immediately sending a correction to \"cave\".There was no reply before he headed off but one minute after he cycled away, a reply landed in his Instagram account.\"Shit weather no can do today,\" it read.Image source, Coroner's CourtImage caption, Some of Noah's clothes were found close to where he was last seen Noah had no internet credit for his mobile phone or laptop, so he didn't see the message on his final bicycle ride.Within half an hour, the 14-year-old had cycled through Belfast city centre and onwards towards north Belfast, but he never reached Cave Hill.His journey would end at a residential cul de sac at Northwood Road. Eyewitnesses who were the last to see Noah said he was naked at the time.There were repeated questions about why he no longer had his possessions or the clothing and shoes he was wearing when he left home.His possessions included his phone and rucksack, which contained a laptop, some school material, and a book he was reading, 12 Rules for Life: An Antidote to Chaos by Jordan Peterson.The inquest heard some of the clothes he was wearing were found close to where he was last seen before he went between private houses and towards wasteground where an entrance to an underground water system is located.Image caption, Noah was captured on CCTV cycling along the Shore RoadCCTV footage provided some brief insights, but none of the recordings explained how he came to be naked.The inquest also heard from Daryl Paul, who was jailed in 2021 for stealing the rucksack containing Noah's laptop.In summing up the case to the jury, the coroner said that CCTV evidence had demonstrated that Paul could not have had any interaction with Noah from the time he left his home until his disappearance about 25 minutes later.Panicked police call and a raw witness statementImage source, Coroner's CourtImage caption, The inquest heard from Noah's mother Fiona about how the two of them shared a special bondAbout four hours after Noah left their home on the day he went missing, his mother made an emergency 101 call to police and that call prompted a major search.A recording of the call was played in the courtroom on the first day of the inquest.The raw emotion of a panicking mother was clear as she desperately pleaded for help to find her only son.She described Noah with affection and told the call handler she had been concerned about Noah's mental health, saying he \"had not been himself at all\" during the previous week.She had concerns that isolation during the Covid19 lockdown might have \"come to a head\" and that she had found Noah crying in his bedroom earlier that day.The boy's mother referred to the book 12 Rules for Life by Jordan Peterson, which she said Noah had been \"obsessed\" by and that he \"hasn't taken it out of his hands\".The sound of that 25-minute recording alongside the sight of Fiona Donohoe listening to it, her head frequently bowed, cut deep in the coroner's court that day.Then came a 90-minute video of Fiona's witness statement to the inquest which laid bare, in stark and emotional detail, her grief at the death of her only son.Image source, Coroner's CourtShe described their \"beautiful, magical bonds\" and how \"his beautiful mind was a gift for me to be in the presence of in his fourteen short years of life\".The court heard how Noah had \"a lust for life and learning\" and \"a thirst for knowledge\".\"We had what we needed,\" she said. \"And we had each other.\"Through her grief, Fiona Donohoe's witness statement illuminated her love for her son and her unstinting desire to understand what led to his untimely death.That was highlighted in one particularly heartbreaking moment when Fiona described how she stood outside their home on the night of Noah's disappearance, looking towards the end of the street to see if he would come around the corner.He never came home.Now the inquest is over, there is some official clarity on some events surrounding Noah's disappearance and death.But much remains unknown about his decision-making and behavioural changes in the hours and moments before he went missing.Many questions – in the absence of conclusive evidence – remain unanswered.Where did Noah go on that nocturnal walk in the early hours of the day of his disappearance, and did he meet anyone?Why was he cycling naked at the end of a bicycle ride that was initially intended to bring him to meet two school friends on the longest day of the year?Why did he end up in a different area after taking an unusual and unfamiliar route?And poignantly, what if Noah had seen the message from his friend saying their planned day trip to the Cave Hill country park had been cancelled?",
