    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-15-uk-set-to-turn-cooler-as-unsettled-weather-continues",
      "title": "UK set to turn cooler as unsettled weather continues",
      "summary": "As temperatures drop through the week and it starts to feel more autumnal, is it now time to put the summer clothes away?",
      "body": "Much of the UK is set to feel cooler from Tuesday, as more changeable weather moves in.Parts of the UK are also likely to see some spells of rain, especially to northern and western parts of the UK.The rest of the week stays a little unsettled with further spells of rain.It could turn more settled into next week with temperatures rising above average for some, though heatwave conditions are looking increasingly unlikely.Tuesday will start off relatively warm and muggy across central and south-eastern areas of England but further north and west it will start to feel a little cooler.That cooler air will be moving south-east behind a cold front that will also bring a spell of rain across England and Wales for a time.Fresher north-westerly winds will develop across all areas bringing showers - some heavy - to Scotland, Northern Ireland and more north-western parts of England and Wales.Maximum temperatures will be around five degrees lower than Monday's values with 17 to 22C across the UK.This is closer to the average for the time of year.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-15T00:24:53.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_weather/1200/cpsprodpb/53e2/live/ff5d9010-b09a-11f1-9e7d-cb2f675dfcb0.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/weather/articles/c98r630jejdyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-15-valve-is-still-figuring-out-how-and-when-to-do-steam-deck-2",
      "title": "Valve is still figuring out ‘how and when’ to do Steam Deck 2",
      "summary": "Now that Valve has finally launched its entire 2026 hardware lineup - the Steam Controller, the Steam Machine, and today's Steam Frame - are we any closer to a next-gen Steam Deck handheld? Valve isn't saying so yet. The company is figuring out \"how and when we can deliver something like that,\" Valve designer Pierre-Loup Griffais now tells IGN.\nValve has been clear for years that 1) a Steam Deck 2",
      "body": "Now that Valve has finally launched its entire 2026 hardware lineup — the Steam Controller, the Steam Machine, and today’s Steam Frame — are we any closer to a next-gen Steam Deck handheld? Valve isn’t saying so yet. The company is figuring out “how and when we can deliver something like that,” Valve designer Pierre-Loup Griffais now tells IGN.\nValve has been clear for years that 1) a Steam Deck 2 will indeed happen, and 2) it’s not coming until Valve finds the right chip. The original Steam Deck worked because it delivered the right combination of price, performance, and battery life, and Valve has repeatedly said it won’t sacrifice efficiency in the sequel.\n“We also don’t want more performance to come at a significant cost to power efficiency and battery life,” Griffais told me in 2023, saying he wanted to see a “leap” instead — but suggesting that leap might not be physically possible for another couple of years.\nEarlier this year, Valve got my hopes up that the wait for a chip was over — when Griffais revealed the team was “hard at work” on the next Steam Deck gaming handheld in an interview with IGN. He wouldn’t say when it might happen. I assumed that was caution, because RAMageddon had already pushed the company to delay and reprice the Steam Machine and Steam Frame headset by then.\nToday, IGN’s new question was: “Has the current hardware shortage impacted the way Valve is thinking about actually putting a Steam Deck 2 into development?” Griffais’ immediate answer was “Not really.”\nThat could be good news for impatient gamers — or it could mean that the company will wait out RAMageddon before it dares intercept the silicon supply chain again. Valve originally wanted to build a “far more affordable” headset, for instance, but instead it faced brutal RAM negotiations to eventually arrive at a $1,059 price tag.\nThe real question is: Has Valve yet identified the future chip that will appear in the Steam Deck 2? Because the company will work backwards from that chip’s availability to create the hardware it lives within.\nIntel’s Panther Lake handheld chip in the MSI Claw 8 EX AI Plus is the first I’ve tried that comes close to the kind of “generational leap” that Griffais has been talking about, but Panther Lake handhelds are incredibly expensive, and the Intel chip wasn’t a silver bullet in my tests. But I bet Valve has seen the future of Intel, AMD, and Qualcomm chips, and maybe a future one will do the trick.\nI hope a Steam Deck 2 will kick-start a new golden age of handheld gaming; I fear it may cost too much for that.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "Gaming",
        "News",
        "PC Gaming"
      ],
      "author": "Sean Hollister",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-15T00:13:47.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/06/DSCF9137.webp?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/games/995256/valve-steam-deck-2-how-and-when",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-14-chris-mason-row-over-reform-s-mega-donations-has-mileage-in-",
      "title": "Chris Mason: Row over Reform's mega donations has mileage in it yet",
      "summary": "The £72m heading towards Reform UK's coffers is transforming the debate at Westminster about politics and money.",
      "body": "The £72m heading towards Reform UK's coffers from two British billionaires is transforming the debate at Westminster about politics and money.In short, how should political parties be paid for, and by who?And, what is a legitimate donation and what isn't?The government is planning to tighten residency rules for people giving money to political parties, to ensure that those making significant donations live in the UK.But it is worth setting out the law, in broad terms, now and how the government hopes to change it.Right now, there is no limit on how much anyone, living in the UK or abroad, can give to a British political party as long as the individual is registered to vote in the UK.But the government has been planning a change in the law for months, which would limit donations from Brits abroad to £100,000 each per year, backdated to 25 March this year. But, crucially, the law hasn't changed yet and some of the key details in that proposed new law - such as what residency requirements would need to be passed to be classified as no longer being abroad - haven't been decided yet.The reason for that date, 25 March, ministers argue, is that is the day a review by the retired civil servant Philip Rycroft was published,, external the first recommendation of which was that there should be \"an annual cap on political donations from British voters living abroad\".On that same day, the then-cabinet minister Steve Reed told the Commons, external that cap would be £100,000 per year. He also added that it would \"apply retrospectively\" from that date onwards, even though the law at the time, and indeed now, has not yet changed.Two big questions arise from this plan: is it proportionate to limit what British folk abroad can donate to UK political parties, given they are arguably less invested in the country than people living here?And is it unfair, even morally questionable, to pass a law, but then backdate when its impact begins?Fast forward to the last few days and along come these colossal donations from Ben Delo and Christopher Harborne, two cryptocurrency billionaires who until recently lived in Hong Kong and Thailand respectively.They have both now returned to the UK.In his first interview since these donations became public, Nigel Farage told me unequivocally that the money from both men was legal under the law as it stands now.But he was equivocal when I asked him if they would be legal under the law the government hopes to pass shortly.\"Both these donations are 100% compliant with the law today, of that there is no question whatsoever,\" Farage told me.Ah, today, I pointed out.\"Retrospectively declaring donations illegal - that simply cannot be,\" he pressed on, adding \"you cannot confiscate money that has been given legally within the law as it stands.\"That is a political, perhaps moral argument, but not a legal one - if the government changes the law, the law of the land will have changed, possibly with consequences for whether those donations are legal.We don't know, because Reform won't tell us, precisely when the two men relocated to the UK. Nor do we know, because the government hasn't decided yet, what relocating - residency - would look like legally. Would it be being on the electoral roll at a UK address? Would it be being resident in the UK for tax purposes?The Communities Minister Baroness Taylor told the Lords that Brits returning from abroad and wanting to donate without limit \"must demonstrate a genuine and ongoing connection to the United Kingdom\" and the government is \"considering how we will strengthen the residency requirements, including ensuring that the length of time spent in the UK aligns with broader government policy\".In other words the hurdles they plan, while still ill-defined, look like they might be quite high - and folk in government, publicly and privately, are leaning into making that point. Farage picked a scrap with them in his interview with me, and the government is hitting back.Do Delo and Harborne clear them, and if they don't, for how long could that delay Reform getting hold of their vast sums of money?The Reform UK leader has threatened that a Reform government would cut off party funding from the trades unions if the government went ahead with trying to limit his capacity to raise money from these two donors.Labour received £5m from affiliated unions last year and £12m the year before, in 2024, the year of the last general election.So, there are loads of questions that tumble towards us after these donations.Should there be a limit on the proportion of a party's funding that can come from one individual?Should there be a limit on what donors based in the UK can give per year, as well as a cap on donations from Brits abroad? The General Secretary of the Unite union has suggested a domestic cap somewhere between £500,000 and £1m a year. Some argue this would compel parties to find their funding via lots of small donations rather than fewer giant ones. But it could also prompt some to call for state funding of political parties – ie taxpayers picking up the tab – which may not prove wildly popular.Then there is the question of spending limits, in addition to donation limits, between elections on the top of the existing limits that do apply just before elections.If I am leaving you with the impression that these donations to Reform, and in particular their scale, are transforming the debates at Westminster about politics and money, well - they are.And yes, all of the political parties are aware of their own narrow, self interest in all of this - as well as their own sense of the wider good.",
