    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-11-brother-of-muslim-policeman-killed-in-attacks-disappointed-b",
      "title": "Brother of Muslim policeman killed in attacks 'disappointed' by calls for Mamdani to sit out memorial",
      "summary": "Zeshan Hamdani speaks out after push back against New York Mayor Zohran Mamdani, who is Muslim, attending 9/11 events.",
      "body": "This year’s 25th anniversary of the September 11th attacks has become embroiled in divisive politics. There are controversial calls for the city’s first Muslim Mayor to sit out the commemorative event, including from Rudy Giuliani who was New York’s mayor on the morning of 9/11.Mayor Zohran Mamdani has said he will attend and his focus will be on the families who lost loved ones.Zeshan Hamdani lost his older brother Salman, a Muslim American New York police cadet, in the North Tower. He spoke to the BBC's Nada Tawfik over his thoughts on this anniversary.Video filmed and edited by Andrew Blum; produced by Pratiksha Ghildial",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-11T04:10:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/08b8/live/ca7d8520-ad62-11f1-9396-b3a11e82bf8d.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/ce8e67l4330o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-11-show-hn-benzi-a-code-intillegence-harness-beating-claude-cod",
      "title": "Show HN: Benzi – A Code Intillegence/Harness Beating Claude Code and CodeGraph",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://benzi.fly.dev/benchmark\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49652389\nPoints: 7\n# Comments: 2",
      "body": "Three ways we've measured it.\nApples to apples comparison: Benzi harness vs mainstream harnesses, 24 GitHub issues, 10 languages.\nBenzi harness on SWE-bench Verified. 78.2% of 500 real issues resolved at under 10¢ a fix.\nApples to Apples comparison: Code Graph's code intelligence vs Benzi's AI-native code intelligence\nEvery task, every attempt, verbatim — nothing held back.\nLearn more about Benzi: benzi.fly.dev/about\nBenzi's KPI (Key Performance Indicator) — source lines read\nEvery harness opens more source as bugs get harder. The question is the slope.\nEach point is one bug; the 24 are laid out easiest to hardest, left to right.\nDifficulty is Claude Code's turn count on that bug — a third-party yardstick, so no\nharness sets its own position on the axis. Lines read counts only what came back from\nfile-read calls; grep and shell output are search, not reading. The figure beside each line is its\nslope: how many extra lines that harness opens per step of difficulty. Hover any point for the bug\nLines read counts only what came back from file-read calls; grep and shell output are search, not reading. The green figure in each row is the lowest of the four.\nThe same 24 bugs in the same order, with wall clock in place of lines read.\nWall clock is raw here — unlike the tables above, Benzi's per-repo index build is\nnot subtracted, so these seconds run slightly higher than the warm figures quoted elsewhere on this page.\nEach point is that harness's most recent solved run for that bug; unsolved and unfinished runs are left\nout rather than plotted as fast. Benzi on Sonnet never solved http-parser and the DeepSeek harness never\nran nats-server, so those two points are absent and neither enters its fit.\nAnd the same again with dollars on the vertical axis.\nPriced at the published per-token rates, same run selection as the chart above it. The\ntwo DeepSeek series run an order of magnitude cheaper than the two Sonnet ones, so at this scale they\nsit close to the baseline — the per-bug figures behind them are in the DeepSeek table further down.\nWhat the axis does show is the slope: Claude Code's cost climbs with difficulty faster than any\nPriced at published per-token rates. The green figure in each row is the lowest of the four; the two DeepSeek columns are cheaper largely because that model costs roughly twenty times less per token. Blank cells are the two runs that never produced a fix.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "tweedler290",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-11T01:22:44.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://benzi.fly.dev/benchmark",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-11-mexican-student-creates-an-acoustic-fire-extinguisher-to-put",
      "title": "Mexican student creates an acoustic fire extinguisher to put out fire in seconds",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.upsocl.com/en/16-year-old-mexican-student-creates-an-acoustic-fire-extinguisher-that-uses-sound-waves-to-put-out-fires-in-seconds/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49652237\nPoints: 220\n# Comments: 79",
      "body": "Ángela Karime Venegas Hernández is 16 years old and has an idea no one else had.\nShe studies at CETIS 78 in Altamira, Tamaulipas, and one day decided to put out a candle without blowing, without water, without anything but sound. She assembled a device with a 12-volt battery, a frequency generator, and a speaker that emits 30 pulses per second. That vibration pushes oxygen away from the flames, and the fire simply goes out in five to eight seconds.\nShe tested it over and over, more than 100 times, until confirming that it also worked on wood, flammable liquids, cooking grease, and electronic equipment. It does not pollute, leave residue, or hurt the person using it. What began as a school experiment now has a name of its own, “Vortex Tech”, and is taking Ángela to represent Mexico at an international fair, where she will present her way of combating one of humanity’s oldest dangers to the world.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "rguiscard",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-11T01:00:56.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://www.upsocl.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/530937_2_6a9ad8c6d4ca2-1.avif",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.upsocl.com/en/16-year-old-mexican-student-creates-an-acoustic-fire-extinguisher-that-uses-sound-waves-to-put-out-fires-in-seconds/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
