      "id": "theverge-2026-09-10-mathematicians-want-proof-openai-didn-t-use-their-work",
      "title": "Mathematicians want proof OpenAI didn’t use their work",
      "summary": "Sam Altman, chief executive officer of OpenAI, during a media tour of the Stargate AI data center. | Bloomberg via Getty Images\t\n\nAnother researcher is challenging OpenAI about the data driving its increasingly impressive array of mathematical discoveries. Just days after a bitter row erupted over whether the company's models benefited from unpublished work, a second mathematician has come forward",
      "body": "Another researcher is challenging OpenAI about the data driving its increasingly impressive array of mathematical discoveries. Just days after a bitter row erupted over whether the company’s models benefited from unpublished work, a second mathematician has come forward accusing the AI giant of unethical and “dishonest” behavior and a lack of transparency about the origins of its training data.\nIn a series of posts on Mastodon, mathematician Andreas Thom raised concerns that interactions he and his colleagues had had with the ChatGPT chatbot before OpenAI’s triumphant announcement may have contributed to its success in the field. One of the 10 results OpenAI announced with great fanfare last month involved Thom’s area of expertise, so-called non-sofic groups, and OpenAI acknowledged that their result built heavily on previous work by Thom and fellow mathematician Gábor Kun.&nbsp;&nbsp;\nThom said he began reflecting on his own interactions with OpenAI after Tristan Buckmaster, a mathematics professor at New York University, publicly questioned whether the company’s AI models had benefited from his use of OpenAI’s Codex. After OpenAI announced its non-sofic groups result, it was widely criticized in mathematical circles for failing to acknowledge recent contributions from Thom and Kun and the company quietly amended its writeup. Non-sofic groups are, roughly speaking, infinite mathematical structures that cannot be approximated by finite ones.\nThom said he was also struck by “OpenAI’s detailed command of our techniques,” which he said were neither the most obvious nor the most promising routes to a solution at the time. He said he wrote emails to OpenAI researchers Sébastien Bubeck and Mark Sellke, also a statistician at Harvard, to ask whether his interactions with ChatGPT were “part of the training data or accessible to the reasoning process” and could therefore have contributed to the result.\nBut the answer did not satisfy Thom, who said it only addressed whether his conversations with the chatbot could be accessed directly, not whether they had entered into the vast pools of training data the company uses to improve its models. “No such qualification, explanation, or evidence was given,” he wrote. “I take this as dishonesty to say the least.”\nThom said researchers aren’t equipped to reverse-engineer OpenAI’s training pipeline to figure out whether their work has been used or not. “Only OpenAI has the relevant data for that.” If the company is going to deny doing this, he said the responsibility is on them to prove that by disclosing all necessary datasets and clarifying various settings and terms setting out how it uses data.&nbsp;\nOpenAI’s reluctance to conclusively rule out any use of user data echoes the way it defended its recent Millennium Prize breakthrough, both in its public messaging and its communications with Buckmaster — who was working on the problems with Anthropic researcher Levent Alpöge in a personal capacity. In the blog post announcing the Navier-Stokes solution, which concerns the movement of fluids, OpenAI flatly denied using any specific user data: “We (the researchers and the agents) did not see any of their work through any means until they released it publicly — in particular, no specific user data was accessed in order to solve this problem.”&nbsp;\nBut it would not conclusively rule out an indirect influence: “While unlikely, we cannot rule out that de-identified data derived from their usage of our products helped improve our models⁠.” Thom said it is the same obfuscatory distinction the company drew in its communications with him. “De-identification may remove a name; it does not remove the intellectual content of a mathematical idea,” he said.&nbsp;\nIn light of recent events, Thom said “Sellke’s categorical answer was, at minimum, unjustifiably broad and materially misleading; looking back it was plainly dishonest.”&nbsp;\nThom said it “would be ethically indefensible” if nonpublic research supplied by users helped to improve models that the company then used to race those very same users to publication, without consent, proper disclosure, or credit.\nOpenAI did not immediately respond to The Verge’s request for comment.\nHis comments add to mounting unease over OpenAI in mathematical circles at what should be a moment of triumph for the company. Its announced solution to one of mathematics’ legendary Millennium Prize problems is an extraordinary achievement that, should it be verified, few would deny. But this was complicated by the unusual circumstances OpenAI said led it to pursue the problem in the first place: It heard rumors online that other researchers had made major progress and thought it would try too.&nbsp;&nbsp;\nThe ongoing incident has left a sour taste in mathematicians’ mouths. Numerous researchers told The Verge they worry behavior like this will push the field into a more secretive state if mathematicians know that even rumors they are close to a big breakthrough could ignite a race with a well-resourced tech giant eager for glory.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "News",
        "OpenAI",
        "Science"
      ],
      "author": "Robert Hart",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T11:00:57.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/gettyimages-2236544323.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.54557577814,100,78.90884844372",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/993263/where-does-openai-get-mathematics-training-data",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-10-top-four-seeds-in-historic-semi-final-line-up",
      "title": "Top four seeds in historic semi-final line-up",
      "summary": "For the first time since 1975, the top four seeds in the women's US Open singles draw have reached the semi-finals.",
      "body": "\"Oh, wow\".That was Coco Gauff's surprised reaction when she learned she was part of a US Open semi-final line-up the like of which has not been seen in more than half a century.For the first time since 1975, the top four seeds in the women's singles draw have reached the semi-finals at the New York major.Now, 51 years later, the stars have aligned once again with Aryna Sabalenka, Elena Rybakina, Jessica Pegula and Gauff all surviving three-set thrillers to create a semi-final line-up for the ages.\"How amazing - it doesn't get much better than that does it?\" former British number one Annabel Croft said on BBC Radio 5 Live.\"It has felt fantastic the whole tournament - I just feel like I've been more enthused about looking forward to the match-ups this Grand Slam than any I can remember.\"It is the first time the top-four ranked players have reached the women's semi-finals at any Grand Slam since Wimbledon in 2009, and it hints at a shift away from the unpredictable landscape that has characterised the women's game in recent years.Since the start of 2020, there have been 13 different Grand Slam women's singles champions - compared to just seven on the men's side.\"I just feel like there's lots of really big names now, big personalities going up against each other and it's really exciting,\" Croft said.\"We've been blessed with the amount of intriguing matches we've had. You're seeing these warriors out on court.\"Top seed and two-time defending champion Sabalenka will face American Pegula in Thursday's first semi-final (00:00 BST, Friday), while Rybakina - who will replace Sabalenka as the new world number one on Monday - takes on fourth seed Gauff afterwards.While Sabalenka is bidding to become the first woman since Serena Williams in 2014 to win three successive US Open titles, Pegula is the only player remaining in the women's draw still chasing a maiden Grand Slam.The pair need no introduction to each other, having faced each other 13 times.That includes the past two US Open tournaments, when Sabalenka denied Pegula a first major title in the 2024 final and fought back to win in last year's semi-finals.\"It's fun to look at it like a rivalry. I think that's what motivates me,\" said Pegula.\"I know that she's definitely pushed me to become a better player, so I always respect and appreciate that aspect of our rivalry.\"Pegula, who has got the better of Sabalenka on four occasions on the WTA Tour, said beating the four-time major winner at Grand Slam level is a \"hurdle\" she must overcome.Sabalenka, meanwhile, said she was \"super excited\" to face Pegula, who arrived in New York in better form after reaching the final in both Washington and Cincinnati.\"I'm loving playing against her because it's a battle, because it's a fight, and I love being under pressure and going through tough challenges,\" said Sabalenka, who is into her first semi-final since falling to Pegula at June's Berlin Open.Pegula added of Sabalenka: \"I know she hasn't maybe had the best results this summer, but I know that she has a lot of confidence playing here at this tournament, and she's going for a three-peat.\"By reaching the semi-finals, second seed Rybakina will become the new world number one on Monday - ending Sabalenka's 99-week reign at the top.But the second seed has described the top ranking as \"just a number\" and stressed that her focus remains focused on winning her third Grand Slam title.\"It's never quite the same if you become world number one having lost a Grand Slam final or semi-final just beforehand, so she will be trying to cap off an absolutely perfect couple of weeks by winning the title,\" tennis correspondent Russell Fuller said on BBC Radio 5 Live.Standing in her way, however, is Gauff, who will have the home crowd's backing on Arthur Ashe Stadium.The 22-year-old American, who won the Flushing Meadows title in 2023 but suffered fourth-round exits in the following two years, said she has a different perspective to this year's tournament.\"Sometimes you're just so focused on the now, now, now, because you want it now, and obviously you want to take advantage of your opportunities,\" she said.\"In the grand scheme of things, [I'm] definitely sitting here in a different mindset and perspective than I was last year.\"It inspires me a little bit more to keep working, as I see how much I have improved in a year.\"Gauff leads the head-to-head 2-1, but Australian Open champion Rybakina fought back from a set down to triumph in last month's Canadian Open semi-finals.\"I'm going to have some aces hit on me and some winners hit on me, but I'm going to keep fighting and give it my all. That's all I can do,\" Gauff said.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
