      "id": "bbc-2026-09-09-hundreds-mourn-police-officer-killed-in-wrong-way-a66-crash",
      "title": "Hundreds mourn police officer killed in wrong-way A66 crash",
      "summary": "Officers give PC Matthew Blades a guard of honour as people line the streets for his funeral.",
      "body": "Hundreds mourn police officer killed in A66 crashImage source, PA MediaImage caption, A full ceremonial police funeral took place in Hartlepool earlierByLouise HobsonAlison Freemanin Hartlepool and Evie LakeNorth East and CumbriaHundreds have turned out for the funeral of one of the police officers killed in a wrong-way crash.PC Matthew Blades, 37, and PC Tom Clough, 38, died when a Volkswagen Passat collided with their marked armed response car on the A66 at South Bank, near Middlesbrough, on 22 August. Five young men were also killed.A full ceremonial police funeral took place for PC Blades in Hartlepool earlier, with hundreds of officers from Cleveland and forces across the country lining the street in a guard of honour.Speaking on behalf of his family, Cleveland Police Federation chair Lauren Somerville said \"life feels unbearably quiet\" without the father-of-two's \"silly jokes\" and laughter.Listen: Hundreds line the streets for PC Matthew Blades' funeralMembers of other emergency services were present at St Joseph's Roman Catholic Church, alongside community leaders and politicians.Cleveland Police chief constable Victoria Fuller greeted mourners at the entrance to the church.Policing minister Sarah Jones and Home Office minister Anna Turley also attended.Image source, SuppliedImage caption, PC Matthew Blades was killed on 22 August while on duty for Cleveland PoliceThe black hearse, led by a single Cleveland Police motorcycle, was surrounded by officers and bagpipe players.On top was a simple floral display spelling out 'dad'.Officers lowered their flags at the side of the street for the procession to pass while uniformed officers carried PC Blades' coffin, wrapped in a union flag, into the church as Amazing Grace was played by the pipers.Members of the public also lined the streets to pay their respects.Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Pipers played Amazing Grace as PC Blades' coffin was carried into the churchImage source, Cleveland PoliceImage caption, The funeral took place at St Joseph's Roman Catholic Church in HartlepoolPrime Minister Andy Burnham paid tribute in the Commons earlier, saying: \"I also want to send our sympathies to the hundreds of people who will gather today at the funeral of PC Matthew Blades, including many serving police officers, and we send our thanks to them all for their service and their work to keep our community safe.\"Somerville said flowers had been delivered to the force's stations and people from Australia, France and America had been in touch. \"The support has been overwhelming and it's been comforting,\" she said.Somerville added even small acknowledgements of respect from the public, such as a nod or a thank you when they see police out and about, had brought officers \"so much strength\".Image source, PA MediaImage caption, PC Blades was killed alongside his colleague PC Tom Clough, whose funeral will take place at a later dateImage source, PA MediaImage caption, Cleveland Police Federation chair Lauren Somerville said there was a \"sense of pride\" among officersSomerville said: \"All of the officers across the country will have felt this and Cleveland is such a small force, so it's personal to have lost Matthew and Tom.\"I think the common feeling has been a sense of duty and a sense of pride, doing everything we can to make it perfect.\"Hutton Avenue in Hartlepool was closed from the junction with Mulgrave Road, alongside St Paul's Road and South Road from the junction with Mitchell Street during the procession.Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Police officers from around the country as well as other emergency service workers were in attendanceIn the lead up to the crash, officers had been pursuing the Passat for almost seven minutes after it was connected to earlier alleged criminal activity, but the pursuit was abandoned 10 seconds before the collision when it turned onto the dual carriageway, the Independent Office for Police Conduct (IOPC) said.PC Blades and PC Clough were in a vehicle looking for the Passat after its cloned registration plates triggered an automated number plate recognition camera, although they were not involved in the pursuit, according to the IOPC.Cole Worthy and Theo Rae, both 17, Makai Saddington, 18, and 23-year-olds Michael Cahill and Jakub Matusiak also died in the crash.Image source, PA MediaImage caption, The force said public support has brought officers \"so much strength\"The force federation chair said Cleveland officers stood side-by-side in uniform at the funeral \"filled with pride and dignity\".\"I've spoken to Matthew's family and he was described as a best friend, a husband, a father, a brother and a son,\" Somerville said.\"He was funny and his laughter and silly jokes made them smile even when they least expected it, he brought light and laughter into every room he came into. \"And life feels unbearably quiet without him now.\"PC Blades taught English at Carmel College in Darlington before joining the police force.The school remembered him as an \"intelligent, kind and approachable teacher\" who \"gave generously of himself to the young people in his care\".Follow BBC Tees on X,, external Facebook, external, Nextdoor and Instagram, external and listen to Radio Tees on BBC Sounds.Get in touchDo you have a story suggestion for BBC Tees?More on this storyRelated internet links",
      "source": "BBC",
