      "id": "theverge-2026-09-09-students-who-use-ai-generally-score-worse-at-school",
      "title": "Students who use AI generally score worse at school",
      "summary": "Students who use AI to help them study tend to perform worse at school than those who don't, according to data from a global OECD educational report. The situation is more complex than it sounds though, with certain types of AI use giving learners a slight boost, especially among students taught to critically assess how well the AI tools perform.\nThe OECD's Programme for International Student Asse",
      "body": "Students who use AI to help them study tend to perform worse at school than those who don’t, according to data from a global OECD educational report. The situation is more complex than it sounds though, with certain types of AI use giving learners a slight boost, especially among students taught to critically assess how well the AI tools perform.\nThe OECD’s Programme for International Student Assessment (PISA) takes data from countries around the world every few years. This year’s study, based on data collected in 2025, is the first to be carried out since AI use went truly mainstream. It tests 15-year-old students in science, math, and reading, and draws from over 760,000 students in 91 countries.\nAfter adjusting for socioeconomic status, students who never use AI generally outperformed those who do in science. How stark the difference is varies though: Students who used AI for specific tasks like summarizing texts they were meant to read, or drafting writing assignments, performed relatively worse, while those who used it for preliminary research or more broadly to “help me learn” saw less of a performance drop.\nFrequency of use has an impact too: Those who only used AI once or twice a year and those who used it daily tended to perform the worst, with better results for monthly or weekly AI users.\nIn some cases, AI use can be a true boon. Students who said they used AI weekly to generally help them learn outperformed nonusers, though not by much. The difference grows among students frequently asked to assess the quality of AI-generated information — with that additional training, all regular AI users for general help learning outperform nonusers. The study authors take that to suggest that “moderate and intentional use of AI for schoolwork and learning” could have positive effects.\n“In the same way that we do not become fit by watching sports but by doing sports, learning does not occur through the consumption of content, but as a productive cognitive struggle of the mind with new material,” wrote OECD director of learning and skills Andreas Schleicher. “Where technology enables or enhances that cognitive struggle, students will advance. Where technology short-circuits the productive struggle of learning, it will undercut students’ development.”\nThe PISA study also looked at the distribution of AI use among learners. It found that AI use was more common among those from relatively advantaged backgrounds. This likely reflects better access to hardware, stable internet access, and paid AI tools. AI use also varied widely internationally: Over 95 percent of Vietnamese students reported using AI tools, compared to just 60 percent in Japan.\nAI users do have one thing going for them: The study found that curiosity levels correlate with AI use, peaking with daily users. For now though, that isn’t always translating to better grades.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "News"
      ],
      "author": "Dominic Preston",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T12:05:01.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/25584700/STK483_EDUCATION_C.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,9.9676601489831,100,80.064679702034",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/991956/student-ai-use-scores-oecd-pisa",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-09-roame-yc-s23-is-hiring-viral-content-editor",
      "title": "Roame (YC S23) Is Hiring Viral Content Editor",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/roame/jobs/KuVVqSh-content-systems-builder-editor\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49625110\nPoints: 0\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "Roame is a flight and hotel search engine for points and miles.\nBooking business or first class with points instead of cash saves travelers thousands of dollars on a single trip. The hard part is finding the seats. Award space is scattered across dozens of airline and hotel programs, it moves constantly, and the best deals get taken. Doing it by hand means hours across a dozen browser tabs, and often days.\nRoame finds those deals in seconds. Members also book hotels through us and get our commission rebated back to them immediately. Roame was founded by Tim and Zi. Tim spent the previous decade covering consumer and travel companies on the hedge fund side. Zi ran data encryption systems at Airbnb.\nWe started Roame after losing dozens of hours hunting for first class award space to Asia across a dozen browser tabs. Award travel is a data problem. Humans shouldn't have to solve it by hand.\nWe're hiring a Content Editor to build and run the machine that produces our video content — not just to edit clips.\nThe distinction matters. We're not looking for someone who takes a brief, opens Premiere, and disappears for two days. We're looking for someone who designs a repeatable template for a content format, figures out which parts of it a machine can produce (b-roll, motion graphics, screen recordings, captions, voice, variants, cutdowns) and which parts need a real human on camera or on mic, and then assembles those two halves into something that doesn't feel assembled.\nIf you've ever looked at your own editing workflow and thought \"I'm doing this same 40 minutes of work every single time, I should systematize it\" — this is that job.\nOne thing to be upfront about: we're in the business of extremely informative, time-sensitive content. A transfer bonus, a mistake fare, an award chart devaluation — these are worth a lot on day one and nothing by day four. Speed is everything. Good and out today beats perfect next week, every time. If what you want is a deeper artistic vision and the runway to chase it, this is not the right role and you'll be frustrated here.\nIdeally, you also know how to grow an audience. We want someone with real instincts for what goes viral and how a channel compounds — not just clean edits, but follower growth, reach, and the kind of content that gets shared. We're starting this as a contract engagement, but the intent is that the right person grows into a full-time, key member of the team who owns Roame's audience end to end.\nBuild the templates. Define repeatable formats for our recurring content: deal breakdowns, route explainers, product walkthroughs, founder/creator talking-head pieces, UGC-style ads. Each format gets a documented structure — hook patterns, pacing, caption style, motion system, asset library, project template — so episode #40 takes a fraction of the time episode #1 did.\nRun the AI layer. Use generative tools for the tedious 70%: b-roll and stock replacement, generated visuals, voice cleanup and synthesis, auto-transcription and subtitling, silence removal, rough cuts from transcript, variant generation for ad testing. You should be opinionated about which tools are actually good and ruthless about which ones produce slop.\nStitch in the human parts. Coach and capture the human-recorded pieces — talking head, VO, screen recordings, phone footage — and blend them with generated material so the seams don't show. Knowing when not to use AI is as much of this job as knowing when to.\nShoot on location. We make destination content — hotels, lounges, cabins, cities. You may be asked to go on trips to shoot it, and to be on camera yourself. This isn't a behind-the-lens-only role: we're looking for someone comfortable being a personality in the content, not just the person cutting it. Travel and hotels are covered.\nProduce the volume. Ship short-form for Instagram, TikTok, and YouTube Shorts, plus longer YouTube pieces and paid social creative. Multiple pieces a week, not a month.\nClose the loop. Watch retention curves and ad performance, find where people drop, and feed that back into the templates. Hooks get iterated, not guessed at.\nKeep the system usable by others. Document workflows, maintain the asset and preset library, and build things anyone on the team can pick up — so the machine doesn't stop when you're on vacation.\nYou're fast, and you ship. Same-day turnarounds are normal here. You know when a piece is good enough to go out and you don't sit on it.\nYou build systems, not just files. You think in templates, presets, naming conventions, and reusable components. You get visibly annoyed by repeated manual work.\nReal editing chops. Premiere / Final Cut / DaVinci / CapCut — whatever you use, the output has to be sharp. Motion and animation ability (After Effects or equivalent) is a big plus.\nFluent with AI video tooling. Generative video and image, AI voice, transcript-based editing, auto-captioning, upscaling, cleanup. You've already built at least one workflow that chains several of these together.\nTaste, specifically for short-form. You can watch a cut and say exactly where it loses people and why. You understand hooks, pacing, retention, and what makes something feel native to a feed vs. like an ad.\nReal shooting sense. Framing, lighting, audio. You can travel light, set up a clean creator rig in a hotel room, and come home with usable footage without a crew.\nA growth instinct. You understand what makes something spread, and you've got a point of view on how to take an account from where it is to where it should be. Bonus if you've done it before, for yourself or someone else.\nComfortable on camera. Or willing to get there fast.\nOwnership. Small team, fast pace. You'll pick the format, make the call, ship it, and report on it — nobody is going to hand you a shot list.\nYou already care about points, miles, or travel (or you're the kind of person who will go deep on it in week one)\nExperience editing UGC and direct-response ad creative, not just brand pieces\nDesign system fluency — you can hold a consistent visual language across dozens of pieces\nLight scripting or automation (batch processing, ffmpeg, API-driven pipelines, agent tooling)\nYou've grown an account of your own and know what actually moved it\nThree pieces of work you're proud of. Links, not attachments.\nOne workflow teardown. Pick something you've made that mixed AI-generated and human-recorded material. Walk us through the template: what was generated, what was shot, what tools, how long it took, and how long it would take you on the tenth one.\nA one-liner on why Roame.\nRoame is Google Flights for your credit card points and miles. We search across airlines to find the best flight redemptions to help users maximize their points.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "zman0225",
